V=[
VP(C,'2–5',S('2','3','4','5'),'“GO TO HER FOR REFUGE”',
 sc('The sage Medhas pointing upward to the Goddess’s glow as he speaks to the king and the merchant in the forest',TREE(30,150,1)+GLOW(150,40,36)+DEVI(150,4,.4,False)+SAGE(110,150,1.0)+KING(50,150,.8)+MERCH(180,150,.8)),
 'The sage said: “I have told you, O king, this supreme glory of the Devī. Such is the power of that Devī by whom this world is upheld. Knowledge too is given by her, the Viṣṇumāyā of the Lord. By her, you, this merchant and other men of discernment are deluded; others have been deluded, and others will be. Go to her for refuge, great king, to the supreme Ruler. Worshipped, she grants people enjoyment, heaven and final liberation.”',
 [('dhāryate jagat','<b>the world is upheld</b>'),('vidyā kriyate','<b>knowledge is given</b>'),('vivekinaḥ','<b>men of discernment</b>'),('upaihi śaraṇam','<b>go for refuge</b>'),('bhoga-svarga-apavarga-dā','<b>giver of enjoyment, heaven, liberation</b>')],
 '<b>The answer to the first question.</b> In Part 5, the king asked: “Why can’t we let go, though we know better?” After thirteen chapters, Medhas’s answer: she deludes even the wise, and she alone frees them. So: go to her for refuge.',
 'The answer to “why can’t I let go?”: <b>take refuge in her</b>.'),
VP(C,'7–9',S('7','9'),'TO THE RIVERBANK',
 sc('The king and the merchant bowing to the sage, then walking away together toward a river, the merchant chanting',RIVER+SAGE(40,110,.8)+KING(110,110,.8)+MERCH(160,110,.8)+'<path d="M180 70 q10 -8 20 0" stroke="#111" fill="none"/>'+T(190,60,'♪',12)),
 'Hearing his words, King Suratha bowed to the illustrious sage of rigorous vows and, weary of his excessive sense of “mine” and of the loss of his kingdom, went at once to practise austerity, and so did the merchant, O great sage. To have a vision of the Mother, the king stayed on a riverbank, and the merchant too practised austerity, reciting the supreme Devī Sūkta.',
 [('nirviṇṇaḥ','<b>weary, disenchanted</b>'),('ati-mamatvena','<b>of too much “mine”</b>'),('tapase','<b>for austerity</b>'),('nadī-pulina','<b>a riverbank</b>'),('devī-sūktam','<b>the Devī Sūkta</b>: the Ṛgvedic hymn of the Goddess')],
 '<b>Weary of “mine”.</b> The phrase <i>ati-mamatva</i> recalls “the pit of mine” in the Nārāyaṇī stuti (Part 16). The story has done its work: the two men are tired of clinging. They go to act, not just to listen. The merchant chants the <b>Devī Sūkta</b> (Ṛgveda 10.125); see Part 19.',
 'Tired of clinging, <b>they go to the river to pray</b>.'),
VP(C,'10–11',S('10','11'),'A GODDESS OF CLAY',
 sc('The king and merchant on a sandy riverbank shaping a small clay image of the Goddess and offering flowers, incense and fire',RIVER+CLAY(110,60,1.3)+KING(50,150,.7)+MERCH(170,150,.7)+FIRE(80,96,.3)+'<text x="140" y="96" font-size="12">🌸</text>'),
 'On that riverbank the two made an image of the Devī of earth and worshipped her with flowers, incense, fire and water offerings. Fasting, self-controlled, their minds fixed on her, collected, they also offered her bali sprinkled with blood from their own bodies.',
 [('mūrtim mahī-mayīm','<b>an image made of earth</b>'),('arhaṇā','<b>worship</b>'),('nir-āhārau','<b>fasting</b>'),('tat-manaskau','<b>minds fixed on her</b>'),('nija-gātra-asṛk','<b>blood of their own bodies</b>')],
 '<b>The clay image.</b> They make her image from the riverbank’s earth: the root of the clay Durgā images still made each autumn and given back to the river at the end of the festival. And they offer not animals but their own blood: the self, not others, is the sacrifice.',
 'A clay image on a riverbank: <b>the first Durgā pūjā</b>.'),
VP(C,'12–14',S('12','14'),'THREE YEARS, THEN SHE APPEARS',
 sc('Three suns and moons passing over the two kneeling devotees; then the Goddess appearing in person before them',RIVER+''.join(SUN(30+k*20,20,6) for k in range(3))+''.join(MOON(160+k*18,20,6) for k in range(3))+GLOW(110,60,40)+DEVI(110,10,.6,False)+KING(50,150,.6)+MERCH(170,150,.6)),
 'When the two self-controlled ones had worshipped her in this way for three years, Caṇḍikā, sustainer of the world, well pleased, appeared before them and said: “Whatever you ask, O king, and you, joy of your family, receive it all from me. I am well pleased; I give it to you.”',
 [('tribhiḥ varṣaiḥ','<b>for three years</b>'),('jagat-dhātrī','<b>sustainer of the world</b>'),('pratyakṣam','<b>in person, before their eyes</b>'),('kula-nandana','<b>joy of your family</b>'),('parituṣṭā','<b>well pleased</b>')],
 '<b>She came.</b> Medhas told them about her; now they <i>see</i> her. The gods saw her in battle; two ordinary, broken men see her after three years of quiet worship by a river.',
 'After three years of devotion, <b>she appears in person</b>.'),
VP(C,'16',S('16'),'THE KING’S WISH',
 sc('King Suratha asking for his kingdom back, a vision of his palace and crown floating above him',RIVER+'<rect x="20" y="30" width="80" height="44" fill="#fff3b0" stroke="#111"/><path d="M14 32 L60 6 L106 32z" fill="#c0392b" stroke="#111"/>'+KING(60,150,1.0)+DEVI(170,20,.5,False)),
 'Then the king chose a kingdom that would not be lost, in another birth, and, in this very life, his own kingdom, the power of his enemies destroyed by force.',
 [('vavre','<b>chose</b>'),('avibhraṃśi','<b>never to be lost</b>'),('anya-janmani','<b>in another birth</b>'),('atra eva','<b>in this very life</b>'),('hata-śatru-balam','<b>his enemies’ power destroyed</b>')],
 '<b>He still wants the kingdom.</b> After everything, Suratha asks for his throne: in this life and the next. The text does not scold him. Worldly wishes are allowed; the Goddess gives “enjoyment, heaven and liberation”.',
 'The king asks for <b>his kingdom</b>.'),
VP(C,'17',S('17'),'THE MERCHANT’S WISH',
 sc('The merchant Samadhi with folded hands, a lamp of knowledge glowing in his heart, chains labelled I and MINE falling away',RIVER+MERCH(110,150,1.0)+GLOW(110,110,16)+'<path d="M60 70 l20 10 M160 70 l-20 10" stroke="#888" stroke-width="4" stroke-dasharray="6 3"/>'+T(50,60,'MAMA',10)+T(170,60,'AHAM',10)+DEVI(110,0,.35,False)),
 'Then the merchant too, his mind disenchanted, wise, chose knowledge: the knowledge that cuts off attachment, the sense of “mine” and “I”.',
 [('jñānam','<b>knowledge</b>'),('nirviṇṇa-mānasaḥ','<b>with a disenchanted mind</b>'),('prājñaḥ','<b>wise</b>'),('mama iti aham iti','<b>“mine” and “I”</b>'),('saṅga-vicyuti-kāraka','<b>that cuts off attachment</b>')],
 '<b>The other answer.</b> Samādhi, betrayed by his family yet still worried for them (Part 5), asks for the one thing that ends the worry: knowledge that cuts “I” and “mine”. His name means deep meditation; his wish fits it.',
 'The merchant asks for <b>freedom from “I” and “mine”</b>.'),
VP(C,'19–22',S('19','21'),'BOON FOR THE KING',
 sc('The king back on his throne, and beyond, a radiant figure of the future Manu Savarni born of the Sun',SUN(170,40,24)+'<rect x="20" y="60" width="80" height="80" fill="#ffd60a" stroke="#111"/>'+KING(60,130,.9)+GOD(170,150,.8,'#ffd60a','#e0a800')+T(170,96,'SĀVARṆI MANU',9)),
 'The Devī said: “In a few days, O king, you will regain your own kingdom. Having slain your enemies, it will remain yours, unshaken. And after death you will be born again from the god Vivasvat (the Sun), and on earth you will be the Manu named Sāvarṇi.”',
 [('svalpaiḥ ahobhiḥ','<b>in a few days</b>'),('askhalitam','<b>unshaken</b>'),('vivasvat','<b>the Sun</b>'),('sāvarṇika manu','<b>Sāvarṇi, the eighth Manu</b>')],
 '<b>The frame closes.</b> Chapter 1 began: “Hear how Sāvarṇi, son of the Sun, became the eighth Manu” (Part 5). This is how: the broken king, by the Goddess’s grace, rules again and in a future age becomes Manu, lord of a whole world-age.',
 'The broken king <b>becomes the future Manu</b>.'),
VP(C,'23–24',S('23'),'BOON FOR THE MERCHANT',
 sc('The merchant seated in meditation under a tree, a calm glow of knowledge around him',RIVER+TREE(60,110,1.1)+MERCH(110,140,1.0)+GLOW(110,100,40,'#ffd60a',.3)+DEVI(180,20,.35,False)),
 '“And you, best of merchants, the boon you have desired from me, I give for your perfection: knowledge will be yours.”',
 [('vaiśya-varya','<b>best of merchants</b>'),('abhivāñchitaḥ','<b>desired</b>'),('saṃsiddhyai','<b>for perfection</b>'),('jñānam bhaviṣyati','<b>knowledge will be yours</b>')],
 '<b>Two paths, both blessed.</b> One asked for the world, one for freedom from it; she grants both without judging. That is the Devī Māhātmya’s last teaching: <i>bhukti</i> and <i>mukti</i>, enjoyment and liberation, are both her gifts.',
 'The world for one, freedom for the other: <b>she gives both</b>.'),
VP(C,'26–29',S('26','28'),'THE END',
 sc('The Goddess fading into light over the river as the king and merchant bow; the sun rising',RIVER+SUN(110,100,30)+'<g opacity=".5">'+DEVI(110,0,.55,False)+'</g>'+KING(50,150,.6)+MERCH(170,150,.6)),
 'Having given each of them the boon they desired, the Devī, praised by them with devotion, vanished at once. Thus, having received the boon from the Devī, Suratha, bull among kṣatriyas, will be born of the Sun and become Sāvarṇi, the Manu.',
 [('yathā-abhilaṣitam','<b>as each desired</b>'),('antarhitā','<b>vanished</b>'),('kṣatriya-ṛṣabha','<b>bull among warriors</b>'),('sāvarṇiḥ bhavitā manuḥ','<b>will become Sāvarṇi, the Manu</b>')],
 '<b>Seven hundred verses.</b> Here ends the Devī Māhātmya, the <b>Durgā Saptaśatī</b>, “seven hundred” verses by the traditional count. The last syllables in the edition are the seed “klīṃ” and “oṃ”.',
 '<b>Saptaśatī samāptā</b>: the seven hundred are complete.'),
]
