st=storypage('river','THE KING AND THE MERCHANT','Ślokas 13.1–29',
 panels('Three panels: the sage ending his story; the two men worshipping a clay image on a riverbank; the Goddess appearing and granting their wishes',[
  ('#e7f4ea','“TAKE REFUGE”',SAGE(72,170,1.0)+GLOW(72,40,20)),
  ('#fff3d0','THREE YEARS',CLAY(72,60,1.0)+KING(30,180,.5)+MERCH(114,180,.5)),
  ('#fde4e4','TWO WISHES',DEVI(72,0,.45,False)+KING(36,180,.5)+MERCH(108,180,.5))]),
 ['<b>The end of the story.</b> Medhas finishes: this is her glory; she deludes even the wise; go to her for refuge.',
  '<b>To the river.</b> Weary of “mine”, the king and the merchant go to a riverbank. The merchant chants the Devī Sūkta.',
  '<b>Worship.</b> They make her image of earth and worship it for three years, fasting, with flowers, incense, fire, and offerings of their own blood.',
  '<b>She appears.</b> “Ask, and receive it all.” The king asks for his kingdom, now and in the next life; the merchant for the knowledge that cuts “I” and “mine”.',
  '<b>Both granted.</b> The king will rule again and become Sāvarṇi, the eighth Manu; the merchant receives knowledge. She vanishes.'],
 ("So who got the better deal?","The text won't say! Both asked sincerely, and both received. Many readers notice the merchant's wish is the one that answers their first question, 'why can't we let go?'"),
 'Two wishes, <b>both granted</b>.')
